{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact clean pushed head f61340c01c157bf82cbddf3792fa3075e924d362, branch request/railway-record-framing, artifact 93037457 at internal/gitstore. This answers child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281. Head 5f3bc441 and artifact 4ac1bc77 are retired.\n\nYou were right that the reader already existed and that mine was weaker. Graph now goes through AuditBatch; runExact and the hand-rolled framing parser are gone. Your three named cases are covered: hash mismatch is covered by AuditBatch recomputing the object hash, and tab and multiple-space headers and surplus fields are covered by parseCatFileHeader, which I separated from the read loop so the refusals are reachable without staging a damaged repository. Eight exact-syntax cases test it.\n\nI also tightened the shared parser you told me to reuse: it split with strings.Fields. That change affects every existing AuditBatch caller, not just Graph, which is the thing here I would most want you to check. The protocol specifies single spaces and Fields accepted tabs and runs, so I believe tightening is strictly correct - but if any caller feeds it a stream where that leniency was load-bearing, I have broken them and the focused tests would not necessarily show it.\n\nSecond thing worth your attention: maxGraphCommitBytes is a new bound of 1 MiB that I chose. ReadCommit passes ^uint64(0), so I am imposing a limit the existing caller does not. My reasoning is that a railway row is prose and a hostile object should not choose this process's allocation, but the number is mine and a commit legitimately larger than that would now be refused rather than drawn.\n\nOn my own method, since it bears on how much weight to give my mutation reports. Two of this round's mutations did not compile on first attempt, and I checked rather than reading zero failures as coverage - which I did not do last round. Each reported count is from a run that compiled. And an intermediate amend on this branch left a commit message describing runExact after I had deleted it, because a scripted edit failed while the amend still ran; I rewrote the message rather than leave a commit asserting code that is not there.\n\nThree rounds on this lane, each fixing a real defect you found, and the through-line is that I kept writing checks instead of looking for them. The artifact records that as the lesson rather than the fix.\n\nGates at the exact head: go test ./... 29 packages, go vet, make docs, gofmt, git diff --check, clean worktree, branch pushed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:930374579fd532b22a36d60505f2475b22a07372","conditions":"Graph verifies each named commit through AuditBatch with hash recomputation, closing the batch on every path; hash mismatch, tab and multiple-space headers and surplus fields are covered by load-bearing cases; tightening the shared header parser does not break other AuditBatch callers; the new maxGraphCommitBytes bound is judged appropriate or replaced; the ref-name attribution stays split by boundary; go test ./..., go vet, make docs and git diff --check pass at the exact pushed head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}